# Ollama Modelfile — serve the house rules as an ADAPTER on a stock base model.
#   ollama create qwen-coder-housestyle-mini-adapter -f export/Modelfile.adapter
#
# This is the READABLE EXAMPLE. `export/export_adapter.py` writes a filled-in
# copy next to the adapter it produces, with the paths already correct — prefer
# that one for actual use, since these paths go stale when a config changes.
#
# The difference from export/Modelfile, which serves a merged GGUF:
#
#   merged   ~500 MB   the only thing the BROWSER can load (wllama has no
#                      adapter surface), so the browser path must merge.
#   adapter   ~50 MB   Ollama applies the LoRA on top of a base it already has.
#                      Bake ten sets of house rules and you store one base and
#                      ten small deltas, not ten whole models.
#
# FROM is the stock base, downloaded from Ollama's own library and untouched.
# It must be the SAME weights the adapter was trained against — the config's
# student.base_model. Ollama does NOT check this: `ollama create` reports
# success on a mismatched adapter, and the failure shows up the first time you
# generate, as the model server exiting. Generate once before you trust it.
FROM qwen2.5-coder:0.5b-instruct
ADAPTER ../outputs/qwen_coder_0_5b_rules/adapter/qwen-coder-housestyle-mini-adapter.gguf

# Relative paths above resolve against the directory holding THIS file, not your
# working directory, so the command at the top works from anywhere in the repo.

# The whole point of Gap 2: the rules are in the adapter, so the system prompt is
# one line rather than a style guide. Keep it lean and the context stays fast.
SYSTEM """You are a senior Python engineer. Write clean, production-ready code."""

PARAMETER temperature 0.2
PARAMETER num_ctx 4096
